rties  for the purpose of bringing  food back to their camps they even had a  semblance of socially enforced laws  leaving members of their tribe to starve  after they committed murder against  their own species  if things had played out differently  this species may have stuck around  perhaps even surpassing humanity and  becoming the dominant species on earth  an entire thriving culture of scp-106  however as time passed the neanderthals  were eventually hounded to extinction  this destroyed the primary food source  for scp-106's species  which if you even have a basic  familiarity with evolutionary biology  never bodes well for a species as the  old man's species turned its attention  to humanity  it found they weren't nearly as easy to  prey upon as a result their species  slowly fell into decline  and eventually slipped into near  extinction the old man  most likely the last of his kind went  into a state of hibernation within a  tree  he remained in the tree for what may  have been thousands of years before  eventually climbing out into a world  that had long since forgotten  anything like him had ever existed the  old man woke up into a strange new world  filled with alien structures and garish  lights he wandered  confused and hungry haunted by the  knowledge that he was the last one  left of his kind he wandered around the  new world until he found what he  described as a nest  likely just a normal human home he  became distressed by the structures and  lights around him  until finally happening upon a food  source that he hungrily  ate this food source turned out to be a  human  infant not long after that he was  apprehended by figures described as the  men in white coats  who are likely to be agents of the scp  foundation he was contained not long  after that  these days his only motivating factor is  the search for pain and anguish  the only things that can give this very  very old man  any semblance of feelings anymore of  course this is actually the least  popular of the three theories  but it does pose a rather disturbing  question if ever there was a whole  species of scp-106  is it really possible that our species  would be able to outlast them  the second most popular theory was that  scp-106 was not only once human  but an extremely important foundation  researcher  namely dr robert scranton if that name  sounds familiar to you  it may be because you've heard of the  famous scranton reality anchors  these devices inspired by scranton have  been the foundation's greatest tools in  combating the anomalous effects of  reality warpers  and if the scranton theory about the  origin of 106 is to be believed  it's darkly ironic that it would be a  reality warping anomaly that would bring  dr scranton to his  unfortunate end the horrors began on  january 2nd  in the year 2000 when dr scranton and  his wife dr anna lang were testing a new  technology  the lang scranton stabilizer this was  the technology that would lay the  groundwork for the reality anchors that  would later be created in scranton's  name  however the stabilizer was ironically  anything but stable  and caused what is known as a momentary  class c broken entry wormhole  scranton was sucked into the wormhole  and transported to a pocket dimension  known as scp-3001  or the red reality many people assumed  scranton was dead  but the reality was far more horrific  scp-3001 which the still-living scranton  and the control pad of the stabilizer  were sucked into was a kind of empty  infinitely extending void the laws of  reality did not apply in the darkened  void of 3001  there was no space no light no passage  of time  it was the absence of all existence  except for scranton who was the one  speck of existence trapped inside as  whatever passed for time went on  scranton could only speak into a small  audio recorder embedded in the console  it became his only company the only  thing anchoring him to some semblance of  sanity  but of course that couldn't 